{
  "workId": "work.homer.iliad",
  "source": "editorial",
  "note": "Hand-authored editorial seed for the margin rail. Chapter overviews are keyed by book number (all editions); passages carry the people and connections shown in the rail, keyed by edition-specific sectionId. The pipeline does not overwrite this file.",
  "chapters": {
    "1": "A plague tears through the Greek camp, and a furious quarrel erupts between Agamemnon and Achilles over a captive woman. Achilles withdraws from the war and begs his goddess mother to make the Greeks pay for the insult.",
    "2": "Agamemnon, tricked by a false dream, tests his weary army by proposing they sail home, and nearly loses them when they bolt for the ships. Order is restored, and the great armies on both sides muster for battle.",
    "3": "Paris, who started the war by stealing Helen, challenges her husband Menelaus to single combat to settle everything. From the walls of Troy, Helen and old King Priam watch the duel unfold.",
    "4": "A broken truce reignites the war when an arrow strikes Menelaus. Agamemnon rouses his captains up and down the lines, and the two armies crash together in the day's first great slaughter.",
    "5": "Diomedes goes on a god-touched rampage, cutting down Trojans and even wounding the gods Aphrodite and Ares on the field. It is one warrior's furious finest hour.",
    "6": "Amid the fighting, two enemies discover an old family bond and part as friends instead of foes. Inside the walls, Hector shares a tender, aching farewell with his wife Andromache and their baby son.",
    "7": "Hector and the giant Ajax fight a long duel that ends only when night falls and they part with gifts of respect. The exhausted Greeks bury their dead and throw up a defensive wall around their ships.",
    "8": "Zeus forbids the other gods to meddle, then tips the scales toward Troy himself. By nightfall Hector has driven the Greeks back behind their wall, and the triumphant Trojans camp out on the open plain.",
    "9": "With the war turning against them, the Greek leaders send an embassy to Achilles, offering treasure and honors if he will return. Still nursing his wound, Achilles refuses them all.",
    "10": "Under cover of darkness, Odysseus and Diomedes slip out to spy on the Trojan camp. They catch an enemy scout, wring information from him, and carry out a deadly night raid.",
    "11": "Agamemnon storms through the battlefield until a wound forces him out, and one by one the Greek champions fall injured. Watching the chaos, Achilles sends his beloved friend Patroclus to find out how bad it has become.",
    "12": "The Trojans press hard against the Greek wall, and Hector leads the charge that finally breaks through it. The fighting reaches the very edge of the Greek ships.",
    "13": "With the sea god Poseidon secretly aiding the Greeks, the battle surges back and forth around the ships. Idomeneus stands out among the defenders holding the line.",
    "14": "Hera seduces and lulls Zeus to sleep so the gods can swing the fight back to the Greeks. Hector is wounded and the Trojans are driven into retreat, at least for a while.",
    "15": "Zeus wakes, furious, and turns the tide once more, sending the Greeks reeling back to their ships. The Trojans close in with fire, threatening to burn the fleet and end the war.",
    "16": "Unable to watch his comrades die, Patroclus borrows Achilles' armor and storms into battle, driving the Trojans back from the burning ships. His triumph carries him too far, to a fatal meeting with Hector.",
    "17": "A long, brutal struggle rages over the fallen body of Patroclus, as Greeks and Trojans fight to claim it. Neither side will yield the dead man to the other.",
    "18": "Achilles is shattered by grief and vows revenge, abandoning his quarrel at last. His mother visits the smith-god Hephaestus, who forges him a magnificent new suit of armor.",
    "19": "Achilles and Agamemnon are formally reconciled, the old feud set aside. Burning with rage and sorrow, Achilles puts on his new armor and returns to the war.",
    "20": "The gods take sides and join the fighting directly as Achilles tears into the Trojan ranks. Poseidon has to snatch the hero Aeneas to safety from Achilles' onslaught.",
    "21": "Achilles' slaughter so chokes the river Scamander with bodies that the river itself rises up to fight him. The gods clash among themselves while Achilles drives the panicked Trojans back through their gates.",
    "22": "Hector stands alone outside the walls to face Achilles while his parents beg him to come inside. Their final duel decides the fate that has loomed over Troy from the start.",
    "23": "Achilles holds a grand funeral for Patroclus, complete with athletic games where the Greek heroes compete in honor of the dead. It is a rare pause for mourning and ceremony amid the war.",
    "24": "Guided by the gods, old King Priam crosses into the enemy camp at night to beg Achilles for the body of his son Hector. The poem closes with grief, mercy, and Hector's funeral."
  },
